WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.690
- Meet Dr. Vivek Murthy.

00:00:01.690 --> 00:00:03.250
He's the United States surgeon general.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.083
What does that mean?

00:00:04.083 --> 00:00:04.916
And what does he do?

00:00:04.916 --> 00:00:07.590
The surgeon general is sometimes
called the nation's doctor.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.050
He or she is the government's
primary spokesperson

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.330
on all matters of public health.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:12.620
- [Vivek] I'm kind of a big deal.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.930
- Recently, I was invited
to trek down to DC

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:16.300
and meet with Dr. Murthy

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.610
to ask him some of my
most burning questions.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.640
Surgeon general, thank
you so much for joining us

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.570
on the YouTube channel.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.190
We very much appreciate your time.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:24.200
We know it's limited.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.050
We know you're doing some amazing things

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.240
here at the White House,

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.130
actually all across the United States,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.810
because your position really
covers all of the U.S.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:34.090
Can you tell us a little bit about that?

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:34.923
- Well, absolutely.

00:00:34.923 --> 00:00:36.330
And Frank, it's so great
to be with you today.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.000
I'm glad we're having this conversation.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.960
You know, I'm privileged to
serve as surgeon general.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.760
It's a job I was fortunate to have before

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.230
in the Obama Administration.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:45.380
And the job of the surgeon general

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.180
is really to provide the country

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.780
with the best possible
information based on science

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.570
so they can make good
decisions about their health.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:52.850
And during my time as surgeon general,

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.870
one my hopes is that we
can first and foremost

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.000
turn this pandemic around.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.549
And that's why I want
to do everything I can

00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:01.240
to make sure people know
the facts about the vaccine

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.970
so they can get vaccinated,
protect themselves

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:03.840
and their families.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.980
But I also want us to focus
and have a conversation

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.160
about some of the other
challenges that we're facing

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.890
that have been made worse by COVID,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.850
mental health, substance use disorders,

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.200
and more broadly,
challenges around equity.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.630
- Everyone knows the
position of surgeon general

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:22.701
for its pop culture
meaning of being on labels,

00:01:22.701 --> 00:01:23.534
(Vivek laughs)

00:01:23.534 --> 00:01:26.150
tobacco products, alcohol products.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:27.240
What's the next label?

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.073
Do we have one?

00:01:28.073 --> 00:01:29.530
Is it gonna be social media, vaping?

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:30.900
Is there one in the works

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.201
that perhaps the YouTube
community isn't aware of yet?

00:01:33.201 --> 00:01:34.034
(Vivek laughing)

00:01:34.034 --> 00:01:35.540
- Well, there's no label in the works-

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:36.550
- Okay.
- ... at the moment.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:39.520
But there are things
that I'm worried about

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.330
that I think that we do
need to raise a flag about.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.100
- Okay.
- In 2016,

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:43.933
when I was surgeon general,

00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:48.210
we actually issued a report
about e-cigarettes in youth,

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.250
recognizing that for many children,

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.950
millions in fact, of kids,
that this was becoming a way

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.540
through which they were
developing independence

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.030
actually on nicotine.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.190
And there are other areas also

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:03.090
where parents are
increasingly raising concerns

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.330
to our office and others around,
for example, social media

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.990
and wondering what are the impacts

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:09.850
on the mental health of my children.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:12.080
And I should just say, lastly,

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.660
when it comes to technology platforms,

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.240
there's also a profound challenge we have

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.960
with misinformation right now.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.740
We see that rampant with COVID,

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:21.600
throughout this pandemic,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.850
particularly now with the vaccines.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.090
And so one question that we've gotta ask

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:27.380
is what's your responsibility

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.900
to make sure that there's
accurate information

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:30.880
that users are getting.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.160
- How do you see yourself

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.670
playing a role as surgeon general

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:36.690
to guide this conversation, movement

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.060
towards taking mental health seriously,

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.520
talking about it responsibly?

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.550
What can we do from a national
perspective here on this?

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:44.780
- Well, it's such an important question.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.130
And I think we actually have
to tackle it on several fronts,

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.360
because we have actually even pre-pandemic

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.680
been really challenged in
the field of mental health.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.440
We've struggled with access
to mental health care.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.880
As you know, in terms of
mental health workforce,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.640
we just don't have enough
people to provide good care.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.380
But we're also not doing enough
when it comes to prevention.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:05.700
There are programs that we
could invest in right now

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.640
in schools across our country

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.050
that would help build a foundation

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.500
for strong mental health

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:12.910
and emotional well-being in our kids,

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:14.670
but we're just not investing there.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.580
But finally, on a cultural level,

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.320
we have to change how
we think as a country,

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.020
as a society about mental health.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.140
We have to see it as an
equal part of health,

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.400
but also not as something to be ashamed of

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.180
if we're struggling
with our mental health.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:27.980
- I've also to read and heard

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.080
that you have a strong passion

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.850
for advocating for the
mental health of providers,

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:35.240
nurses, doctors, pharmacists, lab techs,

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.380
all the people who've
been on the front lines

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.100
throughout this pandemic.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.070
What can we do to help those individuals?

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.680
Because like right now,

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.410
obviously there are
improvements being made.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.410
There's decreased hospitalizations.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:48.980
I have a few ER colleagues

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:50.860
who told me over their
last three, four shifts,

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.740
they haven't seen a single COVID patient,

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:53.730
which is amazing.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.820
What do we say to those individuals

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.240
who have been on the front lines?

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.160
How do we help them?

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.490
How do we give them assistance

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.650
with still taking into consideration

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.090
that this stigma applies to all of us?

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.200
- Yeah, in our house of medicine,

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.530
the sigma is substantial.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.350
And I think, you and I
probably can both recall

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:11.870
days from our training

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.180
when it was very clear that strength

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:16.980
was associated with
things like how many hours

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.570
you could stay up, how many
patients you could admit,

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:22.680
how many papers you could recall
during the morning report,

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.420
or ultimately how many
papers you published.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:26.690
But when was the last time

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:30.090
that we heard about a doctor
or a nurse being promoted

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:34.010
for their compassionate and
empathetic care of a patient?

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:35.330
You don't see that kind of movement

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.470
on the academic ladder often enough.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.540
And so I think part of what we
have to do in the profession

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.050
is recognize that we've
got to shift our culture

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.030
so that people who are
struggling in our profession

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.300
can actually step forward, can get help.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.180
My primary identity is
still that of a doctor.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.670
And I think about our
colleagues in medicine,

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.850
doctors, nurses, pharmacists,

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.640
and others who have been on
the front lines providing care,

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.980
who were already struggling
before the pandemic.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.430
And I worry about the
burnout that we are seeing

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.760
increase in that population,

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.880
because the truth is
we need to serve those

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:07.980
who have been serving us.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.790
We got to take care of
our healthcare providers.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.380
We also know that if they burn out

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.010
at the rates that we're seeing now,

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.410
we are not only gonna have a problem

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:17.610
if there's a resurgence in COVID,

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:19.580
we will have problems caring for people

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.040
for routine medical issues as well.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.410
- You also fall into this
category, into this group, right?

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.040
You're a physician.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:28.470
You experience burnout, I'm sure,

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.270
just like every other human.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.370
What's your strategy for battling that?

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:33.300
- That's a really good question.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.380
And I'm a work in progress.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.470
I have my days where I struggle as well

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:42.040
with burnout and with
exhaustion and it's tough.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:43.390
It goes in cycles.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:44.223
But there are few things

00:05:44.223 --> 00:05:46.680
that I found are very important to me.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.990
One is to make sure that
I'm anchoring myself

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:51.420
in relationships.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.780
Relationships in particular
with friends and family.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.460
You know, when you get burned out,

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:58.150
ironically, what gets pushed aside often

00:05:58.150 --> 00:05:59.520
are your relationships.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.700
As you start to feel
like you have less energy

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:02.610
to interact with other people,

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:04.270
to reach out to other people,

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:05.322
you might feel more grumpy

00:06:05.322 --> 00:06:08.130
or crabby around them as I often do,

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:10.800
and that actually can push people away.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.420
But making sure for me that I'm
spending some time each day,

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.300
even if it's just 15 minutes,

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:16.890
talking to people I care about,

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.930
ask them how they're
doing, listening to them.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:21.230
That's an important source of healing.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:23.120
The second thing that I've got to do

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.880
is to make sure that I
am also pacing myself,

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.340
that I'm taking breaks when I can.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:29.690
And during those breaks

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:31.320
that I'm doing something that renews me.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.330
And finally, one last
thing, which is gratitude,

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:36.150
which I find is an
extraordinarily powerful,

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.460
renewing source of energy.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.150
I try to keep a few moments
at the end of each day

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:43.100
to just remember what I'm grateful for.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:45.870
I was honestly not very good
at doing that until I had kids.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:46.930
And because of my wife,

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:50.770
who is far more resilient
and thoughtful than I am,

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:53.340
she began doing this
exercise with our children

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:54.400
at the end of each day,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.030
asking them what they are grateful for.

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:57.740
And that's actually
inspired me to do that.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:06:59.890
And it's been a really powerful way

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:01.860
to reconnect with meaning

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:04.560
and to feel renewed after
a long, difficult day.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.226
- Absolutely. I'm so glad you have that.

00:07:06.226 --> 00:07:09.370
Because we have this age
of information overload,

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:11.470
constant anxiety, whether
it's from our jobs

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:13.600
or phones or family members, or friends,

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.361
and we start losing focus

00:07:15.361 --> 00:07:17.470
of what brings us true fulfillment,

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.730
which is the extrinsic
factors you mentioned,

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.780
these deep relationships,
meaning, gratitude,

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.270
even looking forward to the future,

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:27.170
even if it's for the next
day for something special.

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:29.410
The pandemic has really
taken that away from us.

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:31.020
You know, for me, the
way that I would cope

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:33.060
with some of my stressors pre-pandemic

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:35.470
was looking forward to the future.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:37.990
So when I was doing my residency,
working a long ICU shift,

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.230
I would say, "Oh, next
month, I have less days

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:41.920
that I'm working," so I
would look forward to that.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:43.300
Pandemic took that away.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:45.320
Another thing that it took away

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.140
was the relationships you just mentioned,

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:49.160
and it's created true loneliness.

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:51.090
Even coming out of the pandemic now,

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:53.820
as some of the lockdowns
ease in the United States,

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:55.370
we're still experiencing loneliness.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:56.220
And I know that's a subject

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.150
that's really important to you as well.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:01.860
What can we do if we are
having some layers of anxiety

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.690
in sort of coming together again?

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:06.580
- This is such an important point,

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:08.360
and this is one of the great costs

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:10.850
of the pandemic that's invisible,

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:12.980
the loneliness and isolation
that's resulted from it.

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:15.620
It's very understandable some
people will feel anxious,

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:17.320
we're all gonna move at different paces.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:19.410
And so the first thing is
just to recognize that,

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.920
to not judge yourself,
but also not judge others

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:24.280
if they're going in a
different pace than you are.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.170
But the other piece too,

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:28.320
to just to remember here is
that if we've learned one thing

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:29.250
from this pandemic,

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.650
is that our relationships
really do matter.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:33.730
They are how we get through tough times.

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:36.660
They're also what sustains
us during times of stress.

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:39.590
And so in the way that
makes most sense to you,

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:41.350
that feels most comfortable for you,

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.290
just start reaching out to friends.

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:44.830
Maybe it's a phone call.

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:46.060
Maybe it's a video call.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.190
Maybe it's an outdoor walk

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.930
if that's what you feel
more comfortable with.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:50.520
Or maybe it's an indoor meal

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:52.550
that you share it together like old times.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:55.800
But building that into your
days and into your weeks

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:57.100
is incredibly important,

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:59.870
because all of us have been
through trauma together.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:02.700
We may have experienced this
pandemic in different ways,

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:05.050
but we've all experienced it deeply.

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:06.860
And trauma requires healing.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:09.300
One of the most powerful
source of healing that we have

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.640
are our relationships with one another.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:12.680
And finally, just remember,

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:16.020
that means also that you can
be a powerful source of healing

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:17.730
in someone else's life.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.420
You don't need to have
an MD behind your name

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.830
or an RN to be a healer,

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:25.990
you just need to be able to
show up in someone's life,

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:29.840
to listen openly to them,
to be fully present.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.470
And through that, you can
remind them that they matter,

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:35.610
that they're seen, that
they're understood.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:37.400
And that is where the real healing begins.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.470
- You have a seven and a half million,

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:41.700
or just under seven and a half
million viewers watching you,

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:43.100
subscribers watching you,

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:44.880
what's the message you
wanna leave them off

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:47.640
about their health or perhaps
about your second term

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.340
as surgeon general?

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:51.880
- Well, first I just want to
thank everyone for tuning in.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:53.760
And I just want to say,
look at this moment

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:55.697
where we're all struggling
through this pandemic,

00:09:55.697 --> 00:09:57.810
one of the most important things

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.390
that you can do to protect
yourself, protect your family,

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:01.810
is to get vaccinated

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:05.570
and to make sure that people
around you get vaccinated too.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:08.370
It is our clearest path to
getting back to our way of life.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:10.470
And beyond this, look,
we do have big challenges

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:11.340
we've got to take on.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:12.810
Maybe you felt depressed.

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:14.040
Maybe you're feeling anxious

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:17.170
about returning to the post-pandemic life.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.050
This is something that's not uncommon.

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:21.780
And so if you're feeling
any of these things,

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:23.280
I want you to know that you're not alone.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:25.310
I want you to know it doesn't
mean that you're broken

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:26.350
in some way.

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:27.780
What it means is that you're a human being

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:30.401
having a very human experience.

00:10:30.401 --> 00:10:33.068
(gentle music)

